; prior: Rust — `Some(false)` is a present value; `if opt.is_some()` is true.
; expectation: [if [Some false] ...] takes the then arm: the Option wrapper
; "says something" regardless of payload. [if-let [x [Some false]]] binds
; x=false and still runs then — payload truthiness is irrelevant.
; principle: the wrapper speaks, not the payload (agent-first.md P1).
; expect-stdout: some-false-truthy
; expect-stdout: bound:false
[fn main []
  [println [if [Some false] "some-false-truthy" "FALSY"]]
  [if-let [x [Some false]]
    [println [str "bound:" x]]
    [println "BAD"]]]
